WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Welcome to Summit One.

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.980
A huge number, a hidden pattern, a puzzling shape: each is

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.180
an invitation to think.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.040
Here you'll build the habits and methods to tackle unfamiliar

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.280
Australian Mathematics Competition problems with confidence.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.510
Upper Primary is the AMC division for Years Five

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.270
and Six. You have sixty minutes for thirty questions, on paper

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:25.930
or online. The questions generally become more challenging as

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:26.270
you go.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.020
Questions one to twenty-five are multiple choice: choose one

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.240
answer from A to E.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.515
The final five need a whole number from zero to nine

00:00:35.515 --> 00:00:36.340
hundred and ninety-nine.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.140
You supply the number.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.620
Marks rise through the paper: three each for the first ten,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.080
four for the next ten, then five each.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.480
The final five are worth six, seven, eight, nine and ten

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.140
marks. That's one hundred and thirty-five altogether.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.500
Wrong answers do not lose marks.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.960
Start with questions you can solve, then return to tougher

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.560
ones. Leave time to check missing answers.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.680
Under the current rules, pocket or scientific calculators are

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.880
allowed in Upper Primary.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.040
Careful reasoning still matters.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.120
Problems can involve number, fractions and ratios, algebra,

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.740
geometry and measurement, statistics, probability and

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.900
counting. You'll learn to notice the mathematical structure

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.700
underneath the story, and choose a method that fits.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:25.770
Take number patterns.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.990
What is the final digit of three to the power of

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:29.350
one hundred and twenty-three?

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.630
The last digits repeat: three, nine, seven, one.

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:36.570
Four positions make a cycle.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:41.110
One hundred and twenty-three is thirty groups of four, with

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:42.070
three left over.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.450
So use the third position: seven.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.710
A remainder turns an enormous calculation into a small

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:48.190
decision.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.910
Ratios become clearer as equal parts.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.190
Blue and gold counters are in the ratio three to five.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:56.850
There are twelve more gold.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.030
The difference is two parts, so each part is six.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.610
Altogether, eight parts make forty-eight.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.210
Geometry rewards seeing structure.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.850
In a three by four grid, how many rectangles are there?

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:12.970
Choose two of the four horizontal lines and two of the

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:14.130
five vertical lines.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.510
Six choices times ten gives sixty rectangles.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:22.730
Five is subtracted from a number, the result is doubled, then

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.570
seven is added, giving thirty-one.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:29.090
Undo the steps in reverse: subtract seven, halve, then add

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.350
five. The starting number is seventeen.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.110
Counting and probability need organised cases.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:38.190
Roll two fair six-sided dice.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.350
Six ordered outcomes add to seven, from thirty-six equally

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:45.730
likely outcomes. The probability is one sixth.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.830
A table helps you count every case exactly once.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.850
Can fifteen counters split into two equal whole-number groups?

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.950
Pair them: one is left over.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:56.890
This is parity.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.570
Along with colouring and things that stay unchanged, it can

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:01.910
rule out impossible answers.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:03.730
You'll learn to explain why.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:08.390
Each lesson starts with the idea: what it solves, why it

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:10.850
helps, and clues that tell you when to use it.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.070
A small worked example makes the method visible before the

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:14.730
challenge grows.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.500
Five quick A or B choices practise useful decisions.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.300
For the rectangle problem, should you count squares only, or

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:24.840
choose pairs of boundary lines?

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:25.980
Choose the lines.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.740
The aim is to recognise the next move, then understand why

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:30.120
it works.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:35.600
Next, you'll practise with support, solve independently, then

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.340
meet a different-looking problem using the same idea.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.920
Explain your plan and check your answer.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:44.620
The numbers and stories change, so you learn methods you can

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.240
carry into unseen questions.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.500
Mock exams bring the methods together under a timer.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.760
Afterwards, review your answers and worked solutions.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.580
Notice whether the difficulty was reading, choosing a method,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.020
calculating or checking.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:01.900
Repair that skill, then revisit it later.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:04.780
You don't need every answer today.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.440
Start with a clue, draw a picture, try a small case,

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:09.620
and build from there.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.420
Each thoughtful attempt strengthens your toolkit.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:13.220
Let's begin.

